WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  thank you so much this is an incredible  honor especially to be included amongst  the other incredible student filmmakers  the idea for this film was sparked when  someone told me that dust from the  Sahara Desert blows across the ocean and  fertilizes the Amazon rainforest and so  without this dust the Amazon could not  exist and I thought that was one of the  most beautiful things I'd ever heard and  the more I delved into the dust the more  interconnectedness I discovered a web  including human health ecosystem health  and climate change as John Muir writes  when we try to pick out anything by  itself we find it hitched to everything  in the universe but we're in danger of  forgetting or just outright denying this  interconnectedness and yet no matter how  many walls we erect or distinctions we  make to divide ourselves from each other  and from the natural world our lives and  our faiths remain entwined  I hope films like this one can be a part  of the movement to act with this  interconnectedness in mind I'd like to  thank my friends and family for  supporting me through the making of this  film I'd like to thank my classmates  staff and instructors from the UC  Berkeley Graduate School of Journalism  including Spencer naka Socko  John ELLs Mike Shen Mark Shapiro and  Chris O'Day thank you to the people  featured in the film who had the courage  to be in front of the camera including  Rob and Wendy Purdy who shared their  personal story and the scientists and  doctors who shared their incredible work  - my co cinematographer Raphael Roy  who's here tonight and to the numerous  other crew and collaborators without  whom this film would not be possible you  know who you are and I'm so grateful to  you lastly thank you to the Academy for  recognizing the efforts of myself and my  team with this incredible honor thank  you

